//
// A HEAP ARGUMENT SHARES THE CALLER'S STORE, AND NOTHING IN THE FRAME RELEASES IT
// (`@FR-F-ParamHeap`, with `@FR-B-View`).  A heap parameter's store is the caller's: the callee's
// write through it reaches the caller, and the frame frees none of it — not the parameter, and
// not a view of it a null-arm `match` hoists into its return (loft#1415).  Whatever spells the
// argument, the place it names is what the callee shares: a `??` argument shares the operand it
// chose, a projection of the caller's record on the null path (loft#2137).  Two sections, each
// with its cells and receipt.
//
// ── 2137-a-coalesce-argument-shares-the-place-it-chose ──
//
// loft#2137, `@FR-F-ParamHeap` / `@FR-B-View` / `@FR-F-Ret` / `@FR-O-NoDiverge` — A `??` CALL
// ARGUMENT SHARES THE PLACE IT CHOSE.
//
// `set(find(p) ?? b.inner)` passes whichever operand the `??` chose.  On the null path that is
// `b.inner`, a struct projection, which is a view (`(B-View)`), and a heap argument shares the
// caller's store (`(F-ParamHeap)`): the callee's write through its parameter reaches `b`.  On
// the minting path it is `find`'s own store, which the frame frees once.  The `if` spelling of
// the same argument was always right on both backends.
//
// Before the fix the inline `??` was lifted into an owned temp, as if its borrowed arm came
// back from a callee that may return its argument (loft#1118, where `(F-Ret)` makes a copy the
// right answer).  Here the borrow is the DEFAULT arm, a place of this frame.  The interpreter
// bound the temp through the runtime guard, which COPIED the place, so the callee's write was
// lost.  `--native` kept the place and freed it at the frame's exit as if the frame owned it,
// so a place reached through a PARAMETER was freed under the caller, which then read garbage.
// The lift now fires only when the borrow arrives through the subject's own return.
//
// The cases vary one axis each: the spelling (`??` with two and three operands against `if`
// and `match`), what was chosen (a field projection, a vector element, a local view, a
// parameter's field), the left operand (a call, a nullable local), the position (an argument
// against a bind, a constructor field and a call that returns it), and the statement context
// of the argument, in a loop that alternates the minting and the null path so a store the
// frame fails to free shows on the leak channel.  `pick` returns its argument or a mint, the
// shape the lift still serves: its returned argument is a fresh copy (`(F-Ret)`).  Every value
// is worked out by hand from those rules.
//
// @falsified-at: 790d88c5c — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 4 assertion failures -> 0, native
//   exit 1 -> 0, native 4 assertion failures -> 0 (LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1 LOFT_POISON=1 make
//   falsify; plain, native fails 2 of them).
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/2137-a-coalesce-argument-shares-the-place-it-chose.patch — the
//   same channels.
// CHANNEL: assertion failures, both backends.
// WITNESS: on the control the interpreter copies the place: `two operands: got 40, want 9`,
//   `element: got 21, want 91`, `through a parameter: got 4040`, `loop write: got 40, want 140`.
//   Native frees it: `through a parameter: got 999, want 909` (the filler `mk(99)` took the
//   caller's freed store) and `loop: got 540, want 4500`.
// HOLDS: the `if`, `match`, bound, constructor-field, returned and `pick` cases pass on both
//   trees; after the fix every case passes on both backends with nothing leaked, under
//   LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1 and LOFT_POISON=1 too.
// ARMED: the leak channel needs LOFT_LEAK_CHECK=1, which the suite runner sets for every cell;
//   the native free reads as a wrong VALUE only because a filler reuses the store, and as more
//   failures under LOFT_POISON=1.
//
// Held FIXED, so NOT measured here: a struct element type (a vector or text `??` argument is
// delivered by its own path), one callee per call.
//
// ── 1415-a-null-arm-source-that-views-a-parameter ──
//
// @FR-F-ParamHeap — a heap PARAMETER's store is the CALLER's, so nothing this frame does may
// release it.  @PLN85's null-arm machinery already said so for the parameter itself: a
// `τ?`-returning `match` hoists its value to `__ret_N` and frees each suppressed return
// source conditionally, and a parameter is excluded from that set because "the null arm has
// nothing of this frame's to release for it".
//
// A VIEW of a parameter is the same fact one step over, and it was not excluded.  A match-arm
// binding over a vector PARAMETER (`match v { [a, ..] => a, _ => null }`) binds `a` to an
// element of the caller's store: the present arm hands that element straight back, and the
// null arm never assigns `a` at all.  The free is therefore inert on both paths — but it is
// emitted at the RETURN while `a` lives in the match block, so `--native` scoped the Rust
// `let` to the block and refused the program it had just generated (`E0425: cannot find value
// `var_a``).  The interpreter never saw it, because frame slots have no block scope: one
// decision expressed twice, and only half of it visible (loft#1415).
//
// The exclusion covers every local whose store is an argument's.  A NULLABLE local bound from
// a parameter and later reassigned from a minting call is not one of them: it COPIES the
// parameter as its dense twin does (@FR-N-Road), so it carries no argument dep and owns what
// it holds — `1422-a-nullable-return-source-owns-its-store.loft` holds that shape.
//
// @falsified-at: 4e3ab5b7 — native exit 1 -> 0 (rustc E0425 on `var_a`, three occurrences,
//   in `head_from_parameter`); interpret was already 0 on the control build, which is the
//   point: only the native half moved, and the leak column is unchanged at `none` on both
//   builds and both backends.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see GUARDS.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.
//   ARMED: `LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1`. This file has no `main`, so falsify routes it through
//   --tests, where the leak column is BLIND — an unarmed run reports `leak none` on BOTH
//   trees whatever it leaks, which records a leak guard as INERT instead of failing loudly.
//   GUARDS.md § A guard that never failed is not a guard.

struct In { v: integer }
struct Box { inner: In, id: integer }
struct H { r: In }

fn mk(v: integer) -> In { In { v: v } }
fn bx() -> Box { Box { inner: In { v: 40 }, id: 1 } }
fn find(p: boolean) -> In? { if p { mk(5) } else { null } }
fn pick(a: In?, c: boolean) -> In? { if c { a } else { mk(7) } }
fn same(r: In) -> In { r }
fn set(r: In) { r.v = 9; }
fn bump(r: In) { r.v = r.v + 1; }
fn get(r: In) -> integer { r.v }
fn check(name: text, got: integer, want: integer) { assert(got == want, "{name}: got {got}, want {want}"); }

fn two() -> integer { b = bx(); set(find(false) ?? b.inner); b.inner.v }
fn three() -> integer { b = bx(); set(find(false) ?? find(false) ?? b.inner); b.inner.v }
fn as_if() -> integer { b = bx(); set(if false { mk(5) } else { b.inner }); b.inner.v }
fn as_match() -> integer { b = bx(); k = 1; set(match k { 0 => mk(5), _ => b.inner }); b.inner.v }
fn element() -> integer { v = [mk(1), mk(2)]; set(find(false) ?? v[1]); v[1].v * 10 + v[0].v }
fn local_view() -> integer { b = bx(); w = b.inner; set(find(false) ?? w); w.v * 100 + b.inner.v }
fn through_param(b: Box) -> integer { set(find(false) ?? b.inner); b.inner.v }
fn nullable_local() -> integer { b = bx(); n: In? = null; set(n ?? b.inner); b.inner.v }
fn minted() -> integer { b = bx(); set(find(true) ?? b.inner); b.inner.v }
fn bound() -> integer { b = bx(); r = find(false) ?? b.inner; r.v = 9; b.inner.v }
fn in_a_field() -> integer { b = bx(); h = H { r: find(false) ?? b.inner }; h.r.v = 9; b.inner.v }
fn returned() -> integer { b = bx(); x = same(find(false) ?? b.inner); x.v = 9; b.inner.v }
fn pick_default() -> integer { b = bx(); n: In? = null; set(pick(n, true) ?? b.inner); b.inner.v }
fn pick_minted() -> integer { b = bx(); n: In? = null; set(pick(n, false) ?? b.inner); b.inner.v }
fn pick_argument() -> integer { b = bx(); w = mk(3); set(pick(w, true) ?? b.inner); w.v * 100 + b.inner.v }

// Statement contexts, N rounds alternating the minting and the null path.
fn ctx_loop(b: Box, n: integer) -> integer { t = 0; for i in 0..n { t += get(find(i % 2 == 0) ?? b.inner); } t }
fn ctx_write(b: Box, n: integer) -> integer { for i in 0..n { bump(find(i % 2 == 0) ?? b.inner); } b.inner.v }
fn ctx_field(b: Box, n: integer) -> integer { t = 0; for i in 0..n { t += (find(i % 2 == 0) ?? b.inner).v; } t }
fn ctx_if_arm(b: Box, p: boolean) -> integer { if p { get(find(true) ?? b.inner) } else { get(find(false) ?? b.inner) } }
fn ctx_block(b: Box) -> integer { x = { get(find(true) ?? b.inner) }; x }
fn ctx_interpolation(b: Box) -> text { "{get(find(true) ?? b.inner)}/{get(find(false) ?? b.inner)}" }
fn ctx_condition(b: Box) -> integer { if get(find(true) ?? b.inner) == 5 { 1 } else { 0 } }
fn ctx_nested(b: Box) -> integer { get(find(false) ?? (find(true) ?? b.inner)) }

fn test_spelling() {
  for _i in 0..2 {
    check("two operands", two(), 9);
    check("three operands", three(), 9);
    check("if", as_if(), 9);
    check("match", as_match(), 9);
  }
}
fn test_chosen() {
  for _i in 0..2 {
    check("element", element(), 91);
    check("local view", local_view(), 909);
    check("nullable local", nullable_local(), 9);
    check("minted", minted(), 40);
  }
}
fn test_through_a_parameter() {
  for _i in 0..2 {
    b = bx();
    got = through_param(b);
    filler = mk(99);
    check("through a parameter", got * 100 + b.inner.v + filler.v * 0, 909);
  }
}
fn test_position() {
  for _i in 0..2 {
    check("bound", bound(), 9);
    check("a constructor field copies", in_a_field(), 40);
    check("a returned value is fresh", returned(), 40);
  }
}
fn test_join_subject() {
  for _i in 0..2 {
    check("pick: default", pick_default(), 9);
    check("pick: minted", pick_minted(), 40);
    check("pick: returned argument", pick_argument(), 340);
  }
}
fn test_contexts() {
  b = bx();
  check("loop", ctx_loop(b, 200), 100 * 5 + 100 * 40);
  check("loop write", ctx_write(b, 200), 140);
  b.inner.v = 40;
  check("loop field", ctx_field(b, 200), 4500);
  check("if arm", ctx_if_arm(b, true) * 100 + ctx_if_arm(b, false), 540);
  check("block", ctx_block(b), 5);
  s = ctx_interpolation(b);
  assert(s == "5/40", "interpolation: {s}");
  check("condition", ctx_condition(b), 1);
  check("nested", ctx_nested(b), 5);
}

struct S { a: integer }
enum Tok { Fn { x: integer }, Id { x: integer } }

fn head_from_parameter(v: vector<Tok>) -> Tok? { match v { [a, ..] => a, _ => null } }

// The NULLABLE-element twin: the binding's own type is then `Tok?`, a nullable local whose one
// dep names an argument.  An exclusion that makes an exception for such a local lets this one
// straight back into the null-arm set.
fn head_from_nullable_parameter(v: vector<Tok?>) -> Tok? { match v { [a, ..] => a, _ => null } }

// The control that always compiled: a LOCAL subject materialises the view into a
// function-scope temp first, so its free was already in a scope that declares it.
fn head_from_local() -> Tok? {
  d: vector<Tok> = [Id { x: 7 }];
  return match d { [a, ..] => a, _ => null };
}

fn a_parameter_subject_answers_its_first_element() {
  v: vector<Tok> = [Id { x: 7 }, Fn { x: 8 }];
  h = head_from_parameter(v);
  assert(h != null, "the present arm answers an element");
  assert_eq("{len(v)}", "2", "the caller's vector is intact after the call");
  assert_eq("{v[0].x}", "7", "and its first element still reads");
}

// The null arm is the path the free was guarding, and the one that would have released the
// caller's store had the free ever fired: an empty vector matches no `[a, ..]`.
//
// ⚠ The absence is read through `??` and through an INLINE comparison, never off a bound
// local.  `h = head_from_parameter(e); h == null` answers FALSE on both backends — a `τ?`
// return that can view a parameter loses its absence when the caller binds it (loft#1421).
// That is older than this fix and unchanged by it (A/B measured with the change applied and
// reverted, identical both ways), so asserting the bound spelling here would pin someone
// else's defect to this guard and fail it for a reason it does not own.
fn an_empty_parameter_answers_null_and_leaves_the_caller_alone() {
  e: vector<Tok> = [];
  assert(head_from_parameter(e) == null, "the null arm answers null");
  d = head_from_parameter(e) ?? Id { x: -1 };
  assert_eq("{d.x}", "-1", "and discharges to the default");
  assert_eq("{len(e)}", "0", "the caller's vector survives the null answer");

  v: vector<Tok> = [Id { x: 7 }];
  again = head_from_parameter(v);
  assert(again != null, "a second call still reads a live store");
  assert_eq("{v[0].x}", "7", "the caller's element is unchanged");
}

// The same question over a NULLABLE element: the binding is `Tok?` either way, so the free
// site cannot tell it from a local that may own — only its SCOPE can, and the free is emitted
// outside the arm that declares it.
fn a_nullable_element_parameter_answers_and_stays_compilable() {
  w: vector<Tok?> = [Id { x: 4 }, null];
  h = head_from_nullable_parameter(w);
  assert(h != null, "the first element is present");
  assert_eq("{h.x}", "4", "and reads its payload");
  assert_eq("{len(w)}", "2", "the caller's vector is intact");

  e: vector<Tok?> = [];
  assert(head_from_nullable_parameter(e) == null, "an empty one answers null");
}

fn the_local_subject_control_still_answers() {
  h = head_from_local();
  assert(h != null, "the local-subject spelling is unmoved");
}

// ── The other half: a source this frame DOES own must still be freed on the null arm ──

fn owned_or_null(c: boolean) -> S? {
  s = S { a: 1 };
  return if c { s } else { null };
}

// A local record source has no deps, so it stays in the null-arm set and the null path frees
// the store it allocated.  Called in a loop: a regression here is a store per iteration, which
// the harness's leak check reports rather than the assertions.
fn an_owned_null_arm_source_is_still_freed() {
  present = 0;
  absent = 0;
  for i in 0..24 {
    r = owned_or_null(i % 2 == 0);
    if r == null { absent = absent + 1; } else { present = present + 1; }
  }
  assert_eq("{present}", "12", "the present arm delivered");
  assert_eq("{absent}", "12", "the null arm answered null");
}
